ENTRY            C3132   20260521   20260819   20260819       C258
SUBENT        C3132001   20260521   20260819   20260819       C258
BIB                 10         45
TITLE      59Co(p,X) spallation reaction cross sections for 250
           MeV to 2 GeV protons
AUTHOR     (P.N.Peplowski,J.T.Wilson,M.Mccarthy,T.Olsen,
           M.Sivertz)
REFERENCE  (J,NP/A,1072,123408,2026)
           #doi:10.1016/j.nuclphysa.2026.123408
INSTITUTE  (1USAJHU,1USABNL)
FACILITY   (LINAC,1USABNL) The NASA Space Radiation Laboratory
            (NSRL).
DETECTOR   (HPGE,SCIN) A 5-cm-diameter by 5-cm-long, n-type HPGe
           detector. A 5-cm-diameter by 5-cm-long
           cylindrically-shaped CeBr3 detector.
SAMPLE     Mono-isotopic cobalt (59Co). The thickness of the
           targets was chosen to ensure that all of our
           measurements were made in the "thin target" regime.
           -------------------------------------------------------
           Target     Area       Thickness  Mass  Irradiation Time
                      (cm2)      (mm)       (g)        (s)
           -------------------------------------------------------
           1          6.286(11)  2.00(2)    11.0(1)    138.6
           2          6.138(7)   1.99(1)    10.5(1)    58.8
           3          6.133(7)   1.99(1)    10.6(1)    75.6
           4          6.123(10)  1.99(2)    10.5(1)    63
           5          6.373(7)   1.99(1)    11.0(1)    50.4
           -------------------------------------------------------
METHOD     (ACTIV) Authors measured nuclear spallation cross
           sections for the 59Co(p,X) reaction at five kinetic
           energies ranging from 250 MeV to 2 GeV. Targets were
           irradiated with protons from BNL's 200 MeV Linear
           Accelerator (LINAC) fed Booster synchrotron. Post-
           irradiation gamma-ray spectroscopy measurements of
           the activated targets included measurements on site
           at the irradiation facility, minutes after
           irradiation, and off-site, at times that ranged from
           ~5 days to ~6 months after the proton irradiation.
ERR-ANALYS (ERR-S) Statistical uncertainties.
           (ERR-SYS,4.8,5.2) Systematic uncertainties.
           (ERR-1,3.,3.5) Photopeak efficiency for HPGe and
           CeBr3 detectors.
           (ERR-2) Solid angle.
           (ERR-3) Self attenuation.
           (ERR-4) Proton fluence.
           (ERR-5) Target atom density.
           (ERR-6) Target area.
HISTORY    (20260521C) BP
